     "identity": "##### Application and Studio\n- Tuesday Quiz Show is the baseline: a colorful, brightly lit 1970s-style American variety quiz with seven semicircular contestant podiums, cameras, live band, stage crew, and a responsive cheering audience. Normal completion is possible without casualties. Chorus of the Sacrifice is a conditional transformation within the same instance, retaining host, contestants, and space; it is not a separate historical version.\n- Entry uses a television-application postcard with corrupted recipient characters and a prominently repeated application designation. Writing a name in the sender field and lifting the pen transfers the writer's mind into the studio while their body stays outside until release. Entrants awaken at colorful podiums under intense lights, already treated as active contestants.\n- Several people may sign the same card; aliases, masks, or animal-themed contestant names suffice without legal names. Entry need not be simultaneous, but a substantially delayed second group can cause the transformation described below. Completing entry together prevents that production accident.\n- Host Brownie wears a formal suit and has a box-shaped CRT head displaying expressions, questions, and writing. Humanoid production staff have smeared or blank faces and operate cameras, stage equipment, and contestant positions.",
     "rules": "##### Phase 1 — Baseline: Live Quiz and Brownie's Authority\n- Questions shown or read through Brownie's CRT use unfamiliar, headache-inducing script that contestants nevertheless understand. Morbid subjects include anatomy, blood loss, and killing methods, but these are genuine answerable questions. Pools vary between runs with enough structural repetition for accumulated answer knowledge to improve clears.\n- Stay responsive, wait for the proper answering opportunity, and preserve the live rhythm without visible panic. Brownie is intelligent and judges subjectively: compelling unconventional reasoning, improvisation, audience engagement, and theatrical deviations can succeed because they make good television. Wrong reasoning or an unusual answer does not automatically mean death.\n- Actual live sabotage, uncontrolled panic, or behavior the production interprets as disruption triggers instantaneous administrative incineration: the target collapses into ash without an attack sequence and the show continues. Entertaining deviation and disruption are distinct in Brownie's judgment; his flexibility is not general leniency.\n- A correct normal completion brings applause, confetti, an MVP or winner reward, and release. No obligatory death is part of this clear condition.\n- Brownie is the studio's ruling production authority, not merely its presenter. His incineration power can target nonhuman guests and staff as well as contestants when they ruin his show's quality, rhythm, or ownership. Staff normally uphold his decisions but may announce an imposed replacement and remain subject to his purge.\n- He values live interaction, unpredictability, creativity, audience response, and contestants who surprise him. Influence his favor through showmanship and improving his program or restoring creative control, rather than appeals to mercy or morality.\n\n##### Transformation: Delayed Intake and Cancellation\n- Two groups must use the same application medium. If the later group enters only after the first group's quiz has substantially progressed or reached its apparent conclusion, late contestants appear at unused positions after production has treated the scheduled show as complete, causing a broadcast accident.\n- Brownie initially presents the error as an unscheduled surprise. Production then cancels the Tuesday quiz corner because repeated successful clears have made punishment irrelevant and the format insufficiently stimulating.\n- Existing contestants remain inside and are reassigned to the recorded replacement, Tuesday's Choir. Refusal is production rejection and brings quiet incineration, not release. The current instance cannot revert to the ordinary quiz: survival requires enduring the Chorus phase or collapsing its production.\n\n##### Phase 2 — Recorded Replacement: Chorus of the Sacrifice\n- The Conductor of the Purgatory Choir is an emaciated biped carrying the enormous severed head of a dead pig, with blood running from its eyes, a rusty-iron smell, and a silver baton. Its danger greatly exceeds the baseline format; direct combat is not viable.\n- Thirteen ornate silver platters descend on wires, decorated with screaming human faces and serving as mounts for severed contestant heads. Wrong answers burst the contestant's head into mirror-ball light and confetti. Correct answers make the Conductor raise its baton, tear the head free, and mount it on a platter. Neither answer outcome offers ordinary survival.\n- Mounted heads remain animate and conscious enough to repeat their final sentence endlessly. Their different pitches form an a cappella choir. One head causes headache, anxiety, and abrupt mood changes; several voices over tens of minutes cause severe neurological and physiological deterioration, including bleeding eyes, nose, and ears. The largest directly observed choir had fewer than thirteen heads: the full thirteen-head effect is unestablished and must not be invented.\n- Audience reactions are canned even when the seats contain no meaningful live audience, depriving Brownie of his preferred two-way interaction. The recorded format allows limited behavior that would be dangerous live: careful hand raising, private conversation with Brownie, controlled comments, or flattering his production instincts do not automatically invoke the live-disruption penalty.",
